<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LC">Proposition <span data-tag="04LC">9.6.1.16</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category, let $W$ be a collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, let $\kappa $ be a regular cardinal, and let $\overline{W}$ be the collection of all morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which are $\kappa $-small transfinite compositions of morphisms belonging to $W$. If $W$ contains all identity morphisms, then $\overline{W}$ is closed under $\kappa $-small transfinite composition (and therefore coincides with the $\kappa $-small transfinite closure of $W$). </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof of Proposition <a data-tag="04LC" href="/tag/04LC">9.6.1.16</a>.</strong>
       Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category, let $W$ be a collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, let $\kappa $ be a regular cardinal, and let $\overline{W}$ be the collection of morphisms which can be written as $\kappa $-small transfinite compositions of morphisms belonging to $W$. Suppose we are given a diagram $X: \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \alpha } ) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which exhibits the underlying map $f: X(0) \rightarrow X(\alpha )$ as a $\kappa $-small transfinite composition of morphisms of $\overline{W}$. We wish to show that $f$ also belongs to $\overline{W}$. 
    <p>
      For each ordinal $\beta &lt; \alpha $, let $u_{\beta }: X(\beta ) \rightarrow X(\beta +1)$ denote the morphism of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ obtained by evaluating $X$ on the pair $(\beta , \beta +1)$. By assumption, $u_{\beta }$ belongs to $\overline{W}$. We can therefore choose a $\kappa $-small well-ordered set $(B(\beta ), \leq _{\beta } )$ and a diagram $Y_{\beta }: \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( B(\beta ) )^{\triangleright } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which exhibits $u_{\beta }$ as a transfinite composition of morphisms of $W$, in the sense of Variant <a data-tag="04L2" href="/tag/04L2">9.6.1.4</a>. Since $W$ contains all identity morphisms in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, we can assume without loss of generality that each $B(\beta )$ is nonempty (see Examples <a data-tag="006N" href="/tag/006N">9.6.1.5</a> and <a data-tag="006P" href="/tag/006P">9.6.1.6</a>), and therefore contains a smallest element $a_{\beta }$. Let $a_{\alpha }$ be an auxiliary symbol, set $B(\alpha )= \{  a_{\alpha } \} $, and let $B$ denote the disjoint union $\coprod _{ \beta \leq \alpha } B(\beta )$. Since $\kappa $ is regular, the set $B$ is also $\kappa $-small. Given elements $b \in B(\beta )$ and $b' \in B(\beta ')$, we write $b' \leq b$ if either $\beta ' &lt; \beta $, or $\beta ' = \beta $ and $b' \leq _{\beta } b$. For each $b \in B$, define $B_{\leq b} = \{  b' \in B: b' \leq b \} $ and $B_{&lt; b} = B_{\leq b} \setminus \{ b\} $. 
    <p>
      Set $A = \{  a_{\beta } \} _{\beta \leq \alpha } \subseteq B$. The construction $\beta \mapsto a_{\beta }$ determines an order-preserving bijection $\mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \alpha } \xrightarrow {\sim } A$, so that the diagram $X$ can be identified with a functor from $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(A)$ to $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. For each $\beta &lt; \alpha $, let us identify $Y_{\beta }$ with a functor from $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( B(\beta ) \cup \{  a_{ \beta +1} \}  )$ to $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Then the functors $X$ and $\{  Y_{\beta } \} _{\beta &lt; \alpha }$ determine a morphism of simplicial sets $Z_0: K(A,B) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, where $K(A,B) \subseteq \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(B)$ is the simplicial subset appearing in the statement of Lemma <a data-tag="04LD" href="/tag/04LD">9.6.1.17</a>. Since the inclusion map $K(A,B) \hookrightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(B)$ is a categorical equivalence of simplicial sets, we can extend $Z_0$ to a diagram $Z: \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( B ) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. 
    <p>
      Note that the linear ordering on $B$ is a well-ordering with largest element $a_{\alpha }$. We claim that $Z$ exhibits $f$ as a $\kappa $-small transfinite composition of morphisms of $W$, in the sense of Variant <a data-tag="04L2" href="/tag/04L2">9.6.1.4</a>. It follows immediately from the construction that $Z$ carries the pair $(a_0 \leq a_{\alpha } )$ to the morphism $f$. Moreover, if an element $b \in B$ has an immediate predecessor $b' \in B$, then there is a (unique) ordinal $\beta &lt; \alpha $ such that both $b$ and $b'$ belong to $B(\beta ) \cup \{  a_{\beta +1} \} $; our assumption on $Y_{\beta }$ then guarantees that the morphism $Z(b') \rightarrow Z(b)$ belongs to $W$. To complete the proof, it will suffice to show that if $b \neq a_0$ is an element of $B$ which does <em>not</em> have an immediate predecessor, then the restriction $Z|_{ \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( B_{\leq b} ) }$ is a colimit diagram in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Note that $b$ belongs to $B(\beta )$ for some unique ordinal $0 \leq \beta \leq \alpha $. We consider three cases: 
    <p>
<ul>
<li><p>
Suppose that $b$ is not equal to $a_{\beta }$. In this case, the inclusion map $B(\beta )_{ &lt; b } \hookrightarrow B_{&lt; b }$ is cofinal (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="03QL" href="/tag/03QL">4.9.1.26</a>), and therefore induces a right cofinal morphism of simplicial sets $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( B(\beta )_{&lt; b} ) \hookrightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( B_{<b} (corollary="" )$="" <a="" data-tag="03U2" href="/tag/03U2">7.2.3.4). Using Corollary <a data-tag="02XQ" href="/tag/02XQ">7.2.2.2</a>, we are reduced to showing that the diagram $Z|_{ \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( B(\beta )_{\leq b} )}$ is a colimit diagram in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, which follows from our assumption on $Y_{\beta }$. 
</b}></p>
</li><li><p>
Suppose that $b = a_{\beta }$ and that $\beta = \gamma +1$ is a successor ordinal. In this case, the inclusion map $B(\gamma ) \hookrightarrow B_{&lt; b}$ is cofinal, and therefore induces a right cofinal morphism of simplicial sets $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( B(\gamma ) ) \hookrightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( B_{&lt; b })$. The desired result now follows again Corollary <a data-tag="02XQ" href="/tag/02XQ">7.2.2.2</a>, since the restriction $Z|_{ \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( B(\gamma ) \cup \{ b\}  ) }$ can be identified with $Y_{\gamma }$ and is therefore a colimit diagram in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Suppose that $b = a_{\beta }$, where $\beta $ is a nonzero limit ordinal. In this case, the inclusion map $A_{<b} $\operatorname{n}_{\bullet="" )="" )$.="" <a="" \hookrightarrow="" \operatorname{n}_{\bullet="" a="" a_{<b}="" and="" b_{<="" b_{<b}$="" b}="" cofinal="" corollary="" data-tag="02XQ" href="/tag/02XQ" induces="" is="" morphism="" of="" right="" sets="" simplicial="" therefore="" using="" }(="">7.2.2.2, we are reduced to showing that the restriction $Z|_{ \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( A_{\leq b} ) }$ is a colimit diagram in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. We conclude by observing that this restriction identifies with $X|_{ \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \beta } ) }$. 
</b}></p>
</li>
</ul>
<span class="qed">$\square$</span>
